      "fragment_index": 6,
      "start": 337.98,
      "end": 405.23,
      "duration": 67.25,
      "text": "Policy efforts are often focusing only on waste management at the end of the value chain, instead of tackling risks, production, and design features at the beginning. So it's essentially focusing on cleaning up harms without addressing how systems are built in the first place. As the environmental co-lead of the policy network on AI, a bottom-up multi-stakeholder approach that merges stakeholder interest towards common goals, our 2024 report, which was presented at the 2024 IGF, highlighted four levers for more responsible and just AI governance, one liability. AI actors must be accountable for environmental and social harms, sustainability, developing metrics and indicators to reduce gen AI's carbon footprint for climate and circular solutions, three interoperability, sharing sustainability data globally and more equitably, and four labor protecting the workers in digital and environmental value chains.",
